From 52668949cc06980db0a9d9919aa0cab30863f586 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Richard Yang <richard.yang@amd.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 17:16:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] nvmesensor: implement in-place threshold updates to preserve
 state

NVMeSensor was not covered by the in-place threshold update in patch
0019. When a threshold property is changed via D-Bus, entity-manager
emits a PropertiesChanged signal which triggers createSensors(). The
original code destroys all sensor objects and recreates them, causing
the 'assertedLog' state to be lost. This results in:
- Duplicate threshold event logs (one from old object, one from new)
- The first log entry is never resolved (orphaned)
- Potential service crash (sd_bus_add_object_vtable: File exists)

This patch implements in-place threshold updates for NVMe sensors,
following the same pattern as ADC/Fan/HwmonTemp/PSU:
- Skips sensor destruction on rescan (only full creation on first scan)
- Updates threshold values in-place while keeping the object alive
- Preserves assertedLog state to allow successful event resolution
- Adds getSensors() accessor to NVMeContext for sensor lookup

Tested:
root@bmc:# mfg-tool log-clear
root@bmc:# busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.NVMeSensor /xyz/openbmc_project/sensors/temperature/MB_E1S_BOOT_TEMP_C xyz.openbmc_project.Sensor.Threshold.Critical CriticalHigh d 0
root@bmc:# sleep 5
root@bmc:# busctl set-property xyz.openbmc_project.NVMeSensor /xyz/openbmc_project/sensors/temperature/MB_E1S_BOOT_TEMP_C xyz.openbmc_project.Sensor.Threshold.Critical CriticalHigh d 70
root@bmc:# sleep 15
root@bmc:# journalctl -u xyz.openbmc_project.nvmesensor.service --no-pager -n 15
